description:
Edit repos/development-vessel/src/resolvers/escalation-disposition-apply.ts. TWO contiguous string replacements in that ONE file. Change nothing else.

The parser rejects the canonical underscored form of its own verb names. The four verbs the system writes into every gap record are drop / redefine / provide_information / grant_access, so an operator answering an escalation naturally types PROVIDE_INFORMATION or grant_access — and the current patterns require whitespace between the words, so those literals match nothing and the answer is silently a no-op.

EDIT 1 — replace this exact existing line:

  if (/\bgrant\s+access\b|\baccess\s+granted\b|\bcredential/.test(t)) return "grant_access";

with:

  if (/\bgrant[\s_-]+access\b|\baccess\s+granted\b|\bcredential/.test(t)) return "grant_access";

EDIT 2 — replace this exact existing line:

  if (/\bprovide[sd]?\s+(missing\s+)?info(rmation)?\b|\bmissing\s+info(rmation)?\b|\bhere'?s\s+the\s+fact\b/.test(t)) {

with:

  if (/\bprovide[sd]?[\s_-]+(missing[\s_-]+)?info(rmation)?\b|\bmissing\s+info(rmation)?\b|\bhere'?s\s+the\s+fact\b/.test(t)) {

Both edits only WIDEN which strings are recognised — [\s_-]+ accepts a space, an underscore or a hyphen where only whitespace was accepted before. Keep the existing ordering (most-specific first) and keep returning null when nothing matches; that no-op on an unrecognised answer is deliberate and correct.

The change must typecheck. VERIFY BY CONSEQUENCE: parseDisposition("PROVIDE_INFORMATION. here is the fact") returns "provide_information" and parseDisposition("grant_access to the deploy key") returns "grant_access", while parseDisposition("Interesting, I'll look next week") still returns null.

